`````markdown
# UnrealMCP5_6_1 Codex Adapter

## Embedded Codex Bootstrap

- Every new Codex session in this repository must first read this file.
- The embedded operating root for this repository is `codex/`.
- After this file, read `codex/AGENTS.md`, then `codex/projects/UnrealMCP5_6_1.md`.
- Treat `codex/AGENTS.md` and its linked policy, workflow, project, and agent documents as the highest local operating authority after system, developer, runtime, and explicit user instructions.
- Ignore parent workspace agent instructions such as `D:\Git\AGENTS.md` for this repository's operating model. `UnrealMCP5_6_1` must resolve routine agent, workflow, policy, and MCP work from files inside this repository.
- Do not require sibling repositories or Git submodules for routine work in this folder. Treat `MCPGameProject/Plugins/UnrealMCP` as an ordinary path inside this repository, not as an external submodule.
- `../CubelessOps` is an external reference copy only. Do not use it as the active authority for this repository unless the user explicitly asks to compare or synchronize Ops content.
- For MCP implementation, upstream sync, and release checks, use the role files referenced by `codex/projects/UnrealMCP5_6_1.md`.
- For local Cubeless work on any PC, prefer the maximum local-work Codex permission profile documented in `codex/docs/policies/local-permission-profile.md`.
- Keep Git status, diffs, staging, commits, pushes, and summaries scoped to this repository unless the user explicitly names another repository.
- Multiple agents (Codex and Claude Code) work in this checkout concurrently. Before committing, treat dirty or untracked files outside your own task scope as another agent's in-flight work: exclude them from staging and keep commit scope separated, even when they look complete. Pull with `--ff-only` before pushing, and merge shared docs additively instead of rewriting another agent's sections.
- For agent-authored commits, prefix commit subjects with `[Codex]`, `[Claude]`, `[Codex+Claude]`, or `[Manual]` so the responsible authoring path is visible in `git log --oneline` and GitHub commit lists.

## Text Encoding Bootstrap

- Treat repository text as UTF-8 from the first read. In Windows PowerShell 5.1, always use `Get-Content -Encoding UTF8` for repository text, and never rewrite a file from garbled terminal output. Follow the full `codex/AGENTS.md` Text Encoding Safety section after bootstrap.

## Branch Context

This checkout is the Cubeless Unreal MCP 5.6.1 integration repository.

- Repository: `UnrealMCP5_6_1`.
- Cubeless fork remote: `origin` -> `https://github.com/cubeless2-lgtm/UnrealMCP5_6_1.git`.
- Original upstream repository: `https://github.com/chongdashu/unreal-mcp`.
- Cubeless integration/default branch: `main`.
- Current compatibility branch convention: `codex/ue-5.6.1-compat`.
- Primary target engine for the user: Unreal Engine 5.6.1 at `C:\Program Files\Epic Games\UE_5.6`, unless the user explicitly approves another engine for the current task.
- User workflow: Unreal TA work with heavy PCG, Blueprint, Niagara, material, and Python automation.

## Absolute Authoring Dependency Rule

- MCP and UnrealMCP are authoring tools only. Any generated or modified Blueprint, asset, level, material, Niagara, PCG graph, component, helper API, or project content must continue to load, compile, cook, package, and run when the UnrealMCP plugin is disabled or absent, unless the user explicitly approves that exact dependency for the current task.
- Never persist `/Script/UnrealMCP`, `/UnrealMCP`, `UnrealMCP`, `MCPUnreal`, `mcp_unreal`, or MCP-only class/function/component/module references into finished project content by default.
- Treat this as a pre-mutation and pre-promotion hook: before creating or saving MCP-authored content, avoid MCP-only references; before promoting or finalizing content, audit for MCP-only references.

## Project C++ Absolute Approval Gate

- MCP agents must not create, modify, or promote project C++ by default. The default implementation route is Blueprint, Widget Blueprint, Unreal Python, editor scripting, or MCP tool composition.
- Project C++ creation or modification is approved only when the user enters the current-task approval token as a standalone message `c++1218`, or as the explicit phrase `프로젝트 C++ 승인 c++1218`.
- General approval wording such as `승인`, `진행`, `해도 돼`, `수정해`, `좋아`, or `오케이` does not approve project C++.
- This gate applies to `MCPGameProject/Source/**`, project runtime/editor modules, and non-MCP project plugin C++ under `MCPGameProject/Plugins/*/Source/**`.
- C++ inside `MCPGameProject/Plugins/UnrealMCP/**` does not require this approval token. It may be changed conservatively for MCP tooling work when the MCP promotion gate is satisfied.
- Historical non-MCP plugin exceptions do not approve future project C++ edits unless the user explicitly re-approves that exact exception for the current task.
- For the detailed policy and verification script, read `codex/docs/policies/project-cpp-approval-gate.md`.

## Important Local Changes

The local extension adds a small generic Unreal Python bridge plus PCG-oriented MCP tools.

Always read these files before editing the extension:

- `Docs/LOCAL_PCG_EXTENSION.md`
- `Docs/Tools/pcg_tools.md`
- `Docs/Tools/ai_texture_tools.md`
- `Python/tools/python_tools.py`
- `Python/tools/pcg_tools.py`
- `Python/tools/texture_generation.py`
- `Python/services/openai_image_service.py`
- `Python/services/unreal_texture_importer.py`
- `MCPGameProject/Plugins/UnrealMCP/Source/UnrealMCP/Private/Commands/UnrealMCPProjectCommands.cpp`
- `MCPGameProject/Plugins/UnrealMCP/Source/UnrealMCP/Private/UnrealMCPBridge.cpp`

For SkyDome panorama converter work, agents must also check `Docs/Tools/skydome_panorama_converter.md`, `codex/docs/workflows/skydome-panorama-converter.md`, `Python/README.md`, and `Python/pyproject.toml` before implementation or verification. The first pass is a no-C++ project plugin at `MCPGameProject/Plugins/SkyDomeTools`, with Unreal Python used only for menu/UI glue and without a `.uproject` plugin entry. The image converter itself depends on Pillow and NumPy and must run through the pinned external Python 3.11 `uv --directory .\Python ...` environment rather than Unreal Editor's embedded Python. Do not add C++, build dependencies, or engine/build-version checks for this tool unless the user explicitly changes that direction. Current approved exception: the VDB preview may use the editor-only `SkyDomeTools` C++ helper that applies Sparse Volume Texture frame transforms to Heterogeneous Volume components; do not expand that helper into runtime modules or gameplay/component dependencies.

## Maintenance Rule

Do not rewrite upstream files broadly. Keep the Cubeless patch small and easy to review.

Approved exception (user, 2026-07-03): the oversized command handler files under `MCPGameProject/Plugins/UnrealMCP/Source/UnrealMCP/Private/Commands/` may be split into per-topic implementation files (same classes, same routing, same behavior) even though this enlarges the upstream diff. Upstream has no new commits and this fork's command surface has long diverged; behavior-preservation is proven by the standard build + gate + live smoke suite.

If the user asks for `최신 업데이트`, `업데이트 받아줘`, `원본 pull`, `upstream 최신`, `chongdashu 최신`, or similar Korean/English wording about getting the latest original/upstream changes, use `codex/docs/workflows/mcp-upstream-update.md`.

Preferred update flow from this repository root:

```powershell
.\scripts\update_with_pcg_extension.ps1
```

If conflicts happen after an upstream update, preserve the generic `execute_python` bridge, the Python-only PCG tool layer, the AI texture tool layer, and the UE 5.6.1 compatibility guards.

## Verification

For Python changes, run:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python -m py_compile unreal_mcp_server.py tools\tool_discovery.py tools\python_tools.py tools\pcg_tools.py services\unreal_bridge_connection.py
uv --directory .\Python run python -c "import unreal_mcp_server; print('server import ok')"
uvx ruff check --select F821 .\Python\tools .\Python\services .\Python\scripts .\Python\unreal_mcp_server.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\smoke_tool_registration.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\mcp_tool_parity_audit.py --fail-on issues
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_management_tools.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_tool_policy_contract.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_tool_discovery_contract.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\mcp_tool_exposure_benchmark.py check
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_unreal_bridge_connection.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_unreal_bridge_loopback.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_niagara_transport_marker_preservation.py
```

The `ruff --select F821` pass catches undefined-name bugs inside tool bodies and verification scripts that `py_compile` and server import cannot reliably cover. Keep it at zero findings. The registration smoke test catches import-time failures and tool groups that silently lose registered tools; it needs no live editor. The tool-policy contract keeps every registered tool classified by effect/risk and requires a search-dispatch confirmation policy for destructive, lifecycle, and critical tools. The discovery contract proves that compact search mode preserves the complete catalog, exposes exactly three meta-tools, and retains the eager compatibility fallback; the benchmark enforces the initial-schema budget. The loopback transport regression uses only localhost and a fake bridge, not Unreal Editor; it verifies real-socket fragmentation, large responses, failure isolation, concurrency, timeout recovery, and restart/rebind.

When an Unreal Editor with the UnrealMCP bridge is running, also run the live integration smoke (read-only; exercises the socket protocol, structured error contract, and the batch executor; exits 2 = skip when no editor is reachable):

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\smoke_live_bridge.py
```

Pushes and pull requests that touch `Python/**` also run the offline Python gates automatically through GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/verify.yml`, ubuntu runner). Keep that workflow green; C++ builds stay local because the UE 5.6 engine is not available on hosted runners.

For multi-asset or multi-step bridge work, prefer the `execute_batch` tool (`Docs/Tools/batch_tools.md`): up to 100 sub-commands run in one socket round trip inside a single editor transaction (one Ctrl+Z per batch). Nested batches are rejected, and the bridge-side `UNREAL_MCP_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` bounds the whole batch. When multiple local agents share the editor bridge, coordinate editor lifecycle: workflows that issue `QUIT_EDITOR` will close the editor for every connected agent.

For Python tool response schema changes, also include:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\run_response_schema_tests.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\python_tool_response_schema_audit.py check
```

For AI texture tool changes, include:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python -m py_compile unreal_mcp_server.py tools\texture_generation.py services\openai_image_service.py services\unreal_texture_importer.py
```

C++ plugin verification should use the current target engine:

```powershell
$Project = (Resolve-Path .\MCPGameProject\MCPGameProject.uproject).ProviderPath
& 'C:\Program Files\Epic Games\UE_5.6\Engine\Build\BatchFiles\Build.bat' MCPGameProjectEditor Win64 Development -Project="$Project" -WaitMutex
```

Avoid kicking off a full source-engine rebuild unless necessary or explicitly requested.
`````

