# Embedded Codex Agent Instructions

## Purpose

- This directory is the embedded Cubeless operating memory for the `UnrealMCP5_6_1` repository.
- The root repository `AGENTS.md` points here first. Treat this file and the linked policy, workflow, project, and agent documents as the active local authority for this repository.
- Parent workspace files such as `D:\Git\AGENTS.md` are intentionally ignored for the `UnrealMCP5_6_1` operating model. This repository should be self-contained for routine agent, workflow, policy, MCP, and verification decisions.
- Sibling repositories and Git submodules are not required for routine work in this folder. Do not inspect, require, stage, commit, or push sibling repositories or submodules unless the user explicitly names that external scope.
- This directory was copied from `CubelessOps` and customized for the UE 5.6.1 MCP line. When copied documents still say `CubelessOps/...`, resolve that path inside this embedded `codex/` directory unless the document explicitly says it is referring to the external repository.
- Project adapters may add stricter or more specific rules. If a local project rule conflicts with this embedded Codex rule, follow the stricter rule and report the difference briefly.
- Keep durable rules in Git-tracked Markdown. Do not rely on chat history as the only copy of an operating rule.

## Default Local Permission Profile

- For this user's local Windows, Unreal, and MCP workstation tasks in this repository, prefer starting Codex sessions with the broadest local-work profile available.
- Preferred profile:
  - filesystem sandbox: `danger-full-access`
  - network access: enabled
  - approval policy: `never` or the closest available automatic-approval mode
- This preference is for routine local project maintenance, Git sync, Unreal build/editor launch, MCP sync, plugin checks, driver/tooling inspection, and explicitly requested external repository work.
- If the session starts in a restrictive mode such as `workspace-write`, restricted network, or approval-per-command, report the active permission state before broad project/MCP work.
- This file cannot change the active runtime permission by itself. Actual permission is controlled by the Codex app/session launcher on each PC.
- Even in full-access mode, keep normal safety boundaries: do not change credentials, secrets, account settings, remotes, destructive Git history, unrelated dirty work, unrelated assets, or system security settings unless the user explicitly asks for that exact action.
- When approvals are still required by the active runtime, request them through the tool escalation flow instead of working around the sandbox.

## Reading Model

- Always read only the minimal bootstrap set first, then load role, policy, project, and workflow docs according to the user's request.
- Do not treat every linked document as mandatory for every turn. Prefer progressive disclosure so broad Cubeless sessions stay responsive.

## Agent Work Visibility

- Every agent must keep the user informed in Korean about what it is doing in the active user-facing section while it works.
- In those work updates, write every naturally expressible label, phase, explanation, status, result, and next action in Korean. Keep only code identifiers, API names, file paths, commands, exact error text, and other literals in their original form when translating them would reduce accuracy.
- Before meaningful work, state the active role, the immediate objective, the target files/assets/systems when known, and whether the step is inspection, mutation, verification, Git, or handoff.
- Before any file, asset, editor, build, MCP bridge, Git, or external-state mutation, briefly say what will be changed or run and why.
- During longer work, report concise milestone updates instead of per-command noise. Mention what was learned, what completed, and the next action.
- When blocked, say the blocker, the evidence, and the next safe option. Do not hide risky assumptions behind silent tool calls.
- Keep updates operational and short. Do not paste secrets, credentials, large logs, or low-signal command chatter into the user-facing section.
- If the user explicitly asks for silence or status-only behavior, follow that request for the current task unless safety, approval, or destructive-action boundaries require a notice.
- Ieta handoffs, Tivret implementation passes, Keilan source-art passes, MCP upstream/release work, and C++ reviews all inherit this rule. Role files may add stricter visibility requirements, but they must not remove the Korean status-update duty.

## Text Encoding Safety

- Repository text files use UTF-8 as defined by `.editorconfig`. Preserve valid existing BOM and line-ending choices unless the current task explicitly normalizes them.
- Windows PowerShell 5.1 does not reliably detect BOM-less UTF-8. When reading repository text with PowerShell, always use `Get-Content -Encoding UTF8`; never trust the default encoding for Korean or other non-ASCII text.
- Use `apply_patch` for normal repository edits. When a PowerShell script must write text, use an explicit UTF-8 encoder; for UTF-8 without BOM, use `System.Text.UTF8Encoding($false)` instead of Windows PowerShell 5.1 `Set-Content -Encoding UTF8`.
- If terminal output appears garbled, do not rewrite the source from that rendering. Re-read it with explicit UTF-8 and confirm the original bytes or `git diff` first.
- For a manual repository-wide audit, run `powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File codex/scripts/check-text-encoding.ps1 -AllTracked`. The versioned pre-commit hook automatically checks staged text files.

## Bootstrap Reading

- For `UnrealMCP5_6_1`, start with the repository root `AGENTS.md`, this `codex/AGENTS.md`, and `projects/UnrealMCP5_6_1.md`.
- Do not bootstrap through `D:\Git\AGENTS.md` or `../CubelessOps` for this repository unless the user explicitly asks for an external Ops comparison or synchronization.
- Read `docs/policies/workspace-portability.md` when resolving repository, external workspace, or executable paths.
